      "title": "Intercity Road Transport in Iran: Journeys and Disruption",
      "description": "Explore documented aspects of Iran’s intercity road travel, including road information and the role of international coaches during flight disruption."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "road-information",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The information behind a journey",
        "content": "An institutional presentation delivered on behalf of Iran’s Road Maintenance and Transportation Organization describes the National Road Management Center’s work on weather, road closures, work zones, and incidents. It identifies the 141 information service among the channels used to communicate road conditions. This documents an information function supporting road journeys; it is not a passenger timetable or evidence that a particular departure is operating. The presentation is historical, and its descriptions should not be read as a verification of current service availability. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disruption-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Coaches when flights stop",
        "content": "Reporting from Istanbul in June 2025 provides a concrete example of road transport adapting to disruption. Following airspace closures during the Israel–Iran conflict, travelers who had expected to fly to Iran instead boarded coaches in Istanbul. The National interviewed drivers, travel managers, and passengers, including people whose original journeys had begun outside Turkey. [s2]\n\nThe account reveals more than a substitution between vehicles. Passengers described interrupted itineraries, difficulty contacting family, and uncertainty about returning. Coach travel linked an international arrival point to an overland route home, while imposing a much longer journey. These interviews document experiences during a specific crisis; they do not measure normal national demand or establish a permanent route schedule. [s2]\n\nFor understanding intercity transport, the useful distinction is between the existence of a road connection and the availability of a usable passenger service. The reported departures establish that coaches operated in that episode. They do not establish present fares, border procedures, ticket availability, or journey times. Those details require dated operator and border-authority evidence before they can support a current travel account. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
